A seed farm in South Carolina
Farming today means juggling weather shifts, equipment upkeep, labor planning, and tight timelines–with decisions that can’t wait.

Rachael Sharp is preparing to take over Sharp & Sharp Certified Seed, her family’s farm in Allendale, South Carolina. Along with the land, she’s inheriting decades of hard-earned knowledge, including her father’s handwritten crop ledger, kept since 1971: “It’s so much data, it almost scares you away.” Rather than letting that history stay locked in notebooks, Rachael decided to turn it into something she could use day to day, digitizing it and making it searchable with ChatGPT.

“Every single crop we’ve ever planted is now accessible,” she explains. What used to live in memory or on paper is now something she can reference instantly—past planting dates, yields, and practices, all in one place. It’s less about preserving records for their own sake than about continuity: carrying a lifetime of experience forward as she steps into a new role.

Rachael and Don Sharp using ChatGPT to troubleshoot a combine. 

Don Sharp was skeptical of AI until his daughter Rachael began using ChatGPT to help out on the farm.

“Before, I would wonder, where can I get this, who can help me with that. And now it’s like, okay, I can do this.”
Rachael Sharp, Sharp & Sharp Certified Seed
What used to take phone calls, guesswork, or delaying the job, now gets resolved in real time with a simple question.

Day to day, ChatGPT has become part of the farm’s workflow. Riding a combine or walking the fields, Rachael uses voice mode to log loads, check details, and keep moving. When soybeans looked stressed, she snapped a photo and asked ChatGPT what was wrong. When her father needed to double-check a tractor part mid-repair, she confirmed it on the spot. Tasks that once required stopping, remembering, or circling back now happen as the work unfolds.

With less time spent tracking information, Rachael can focus on decisions that actually move the farm forward. The experience and judgment were always there; what has changed is how quickly she can act on them. The Sharp family has seen the number of certified seed farms in South Carolina plunge from over 200 to just seven in recent years. In that kind of landscape, being able to act with confidence, grounded in decades of firsthand knowledge, is how you keep building forward.

