A tamale shop in California
Learning how to grow while keeping a family business true to itself.

The Original Tamale Co. began in a garage in California. The first generation made tamales by hand, selling them out of the trunks of their cars; the second generation turned the business into a factory. Today, the third generation are finding ways to modernize the business—deliberately and on their own terms.

Christian Ortega grew up inside the company and now helps with marketing and operations. Farmers markets are a major part of the business, but with dozens happening across Los Angeles and hundreds across California, customers often struggled to find where they’d be on any given day. Christian decided he wanted to build a searchable farmers market tracker for the website, though he had never written a line of code. With ChatGPT, he built and launched it the same afternoon, making it easier for customers to find them, and easier for the family to keep up.

“I had the idea, made it that same afternoon, and put it on the website. I didn’t need to wait on anyone. I could just do it.”
Christian Ortega, The Original Tamale Co.

The Original Tamale Co. storefront in Pacoima, CA.

Using ChatGPT, Christian Ortega creates viral content for the company’s social media account.

Finding confidence in your voice

For Xochitl Ortega, who runs the business alongside her siblings, the work has always been deeply personal. The business is built on family, responsibility, and showing up — often before sunrise, often under pressure. English is her second language, and for years that added a layer of pressure to moments that already mattered: addressing employees, handling sensitive situations, or representing the company publicly. “Sometimes you know exactly what you want to say,” she explains, “but you can’t find the right words in English. You’re worried it’ll come out the wrong way.”

She uses ChatGPT to shape what she wants to say before she says it. Often starting in Spanish, she works through tone and clarity until it feels right — not just translated, but expressed the way she intends. The result isn’t polish for its own sake; it’s confidence. “I feel like I just went to university,” Xochitl says. “Like I went to a seminar, and now I can speak.” Conversations that once took days of second-guessing now happen in the moment.

What changed wasn’t how Xochitl leads, but how quickly she can act on what she already knows. In a business built on family and responsibility, the ability to respond clearly and without hesitation has made growth feel possible.

