A salvage yard in Nevada
To keep an 86-year-old salvage yard running, you have to solve problems as fast as they show up.

Salvage yards are unpredictable by nature. Loads arrive mislabeled. Machines break mid-shift. Customers ask technical questions across industries. For Richard Lane, a manager at Reno Salvage, the job is less about long-term planning and more about constant decision-making in real time.

Richard uses ChatGPT as a practical tool to unblock work as it happens. When a plasma cutting table malfunctioned, instead of pausing operations for days until he could get the right technician to come out, he described the issue to ChatGPT and got a quick troubleshooting step that solved the problem. When the yard needed to create a comprehensive part-numbering system of over 1,000 products–something that the yard had repeatedly put off, as it would take weeks–he used ChatGPT to organize it in a single afternoon. Now, his employees can work smoother, faster, and safer.

Instead of pausing operations to wait for technicians, the metal yard began to use ChatGPT for troubleshooting their equipment.

